    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3d2dfcd8daaa9e94db6247df8fb0ffc5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1767,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cI thought I should hand this (consultant coach) over to someone else. I thought I can leave after a successful World Cup. I am extremely sad. I have not officially told Sri Lanka Cricket. I am waiting to discuss it with them\u201d, Jayasuriya said at a post-match Press conference after his team lost to Pakistan on Saturday night that ended the team\u2019s unsuccessful T20 World Cup campaign.\n\nIt was followed by two Probe Reports compiled by retired Supreme Court judges and another by a Cabinet sub-committee calling for the establishment of a Board of Directors to run the administration of Sri Lanka Cricket which they said was the only way to clean up the mess.\n\nRecord breaking bowler Muttiah Muralidaran, Sidat Wettimuny a man of impeccable integrity, former captain Michael Tissera and former cricket secretaries S. Skandakumar and Kushil Gunasekera are some of the surviving members who signed the Petition in 2021.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka\u2019s cricket is seriously ill and requires immediate surgery. The Constitution is severely conflicted between clubs and country. This system encourages the office-bearers to take decisions mostly to satisfy its voter base (clubs) causing the playing standards of Sri Lanka cricket to deteriorate\u201d, the Petition to the Court of Appeal stated.\n\nEven Parliament could not enforce its own unanimous vote for change at SLC that resulted in the chief crusader for an overhaul in the administration of cricket, Sports Minister Roshan Ranasinghe, shown the door by the then President Ranil Wickremasinghe.\n\nBut most baffling and paradoxical is the inaction of current President Anura Kumara Dissanayake to implement change at SLC despite being a vocal critic against the conduct of cricket officials when he was an Opposition law-maker."
  },
